From c172eb98d6a2527194d71456a9d664d37173077f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 01:38:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Host threads: setup loops shared between the threads of
 OpenMP (option enable-openmp)

On a GPU, a process holds the part of the mesh of a whole GPU and has
several cores of the host, which wait during the kernels. The setups made
once on the host are loops over the nodes or over the rows of the matrices
of this process: they ran on one of these cores.

`batch::internals::forEachThreadRange` (internal header
`src/Batch/HostThreads.hxx`) calls a function on consecutive ranges covering
the indices of a loop. With the new CMake option `enable-openmp` (`OFF` by
default), each thread of OpenMP treats one range; without it, the function
is called once on the whole range, without directive nor runtime. A call
only writes the values of its range, and declares its own work arrays: the
results do not depend on the number of threads. An exception thrown by a
call is thrown again when all the calls returned.

The number of threads and their placement are the ones of OpenMP
(`OMP_NUM_THREADS`, by default the processors given to the process by its
launcher, `OMP_PROC_BIND`, `OMP_PLACES`): nothing is specific to mfem-mgis
nor to a machine. With the option, OpenMP is searched, the library is
compiled with it and privately linked to its runtime, which the
configuration file searches for the programs linked to the static library,
and MPI is initialized with the level `MPI_THREAD_FUNNELED` (the threads do
not call MPI). MFEM and hypre do not need OpenMP, and the sources compiled
as CUDA or HIP code are not concerned.

HostThreadsTest: ranges which are not empty, follow each other, have the
same size up to one index and cover the indices once, for one, 3 and 24
threads, among which more threads than indices; no call without index;
indices beyond the range of `int`; exception thrown again. The number of
ranges of a loop is the number of threads with the option, one without it.
